{#
A save that was refused, put back into the dialog the user is still looking at.

`update` and not `replace`: the target is the <turbo-frame id="modal"> in
_partials/_modal.html.twig, which carries the flex classes that make the dialog
scroll its body and pin its footer. `replace` would swap that element for the
one _event_modal.html.twig renders — same id, none of the classes — and the
dialog would lose its layout the first time anybody mistyped a date. `update`
changes the children only, which is exactly what a normal frame navigation does.

A stream rather than a frame render, because the form posts with
data-turbo-frame="_top" and a `_top` submission has no frame for Turbo to put a
failed response in. Turbo's StreamObserver watches turbo:before-fetch-response
for this content type on EVERY response regardless of status, which is what lets
one form navigate the whole page on success and update one frame on failure.

The status is 422 and that matters independently: Turbo treats a 2xx form
response that did not redirect as an error, and the browser must not record this
as a successful navigation.
#}
<turbo-stream action="update" target="modal">
    <template>
        {{ include('calendar/_event_editor.html.twig') }}
    </template>
</turbo-stream>
